WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.360 --> 00:00:07.913
- Hello, everyone.

00:00:08.810 --> 00:00:11.470
Thank you all for being here today.

00:00:11.470 --> 00:00:12.880
My name for those of you who don't know me

00:00:12.880 --> 00:00:13.970
is Matthew Tanico.

00:00:13.970 --> 00:00:16.530
I'm the assistant dean
for Graduate Student Life

00:00:16.530 --> 00:00:18.870
in the Graduate School
of Arts and Sciences.

00:00:18.870 --> 00:00:20.410
And I'm excited to welcome you all

00:00:20.410 --> 00:00:22.763
to this Interdisciplinary
Humanities Symposium.

00:00:23.960 --> 00:00:25.720
I wanna begin by acknowledging

00:00:25.720 --> 00:00:27.350
that the land upon which we are located

00:00:27.350 --> 00:00:29.410
is occupied territory.

00:00:29.410 --> 00:00:31.720
It is the traditional
land and an ancestral home

00:00:31.720 --> 00:00:35.430
to Native American indigenous
and first nations peoples,

00:00:35.430 --> 00:00:40.350
including Mohegan
Mashantucket Pequot, Eastern Pequot

00:00:40.350 --> 00:00:43.890
Schaghticoke, Golden Hill Paugussett, Niantic,

00:00:43.890 --> 00:00:47.180
and the Quinnipiac and other
Algonquin speaking peoples.

00:00:47.180 --> 00:00:48.930
We take a moment to recognize and honor

00:00:48.930 --> 00:00:50.890
the current indigenous population,

00:00:50.890 --> 00:00:52.540
knowing that this is our present

00:00:52.540 --> 00:00:54.333
as much as it is our past.

00:00:58.540 --> 00:01:00.520
There are a number of
reasons why graduate students

00:01:00.520 --> 00:01:01.980
choose to come to Yale.

00:01:01.980 --> 00:01:05.090
And among the top are our
distinguished faculty,

00:01:05.090 --> 00:01:06.710
our collaborative community,

00:01:06.710 --> 00:01:08.940
and the many resources that allow students

00:01:08.940 --> 00:01:11.160
to carry out their scholarship.

00:01:11.160 --> 00:01:13.430
This symposium is intended to highlight

00:01:13.430 --> 00:01:15.080
the impressive collection of resources

00:01:15.080 --> 00:01:17.660
dedicated to the humanities on campus,

00:01:17.660 --> 00:01:20.510
not just within the traditional
departments and programs,

00:01:20.510 --> 00:01:24.260
but reaching out across
and beyond the division.

00:01:24.260 --> 00:01:26.910
It is a showcase particularly for first

00:01:26.910 --> 00:01:28.280
and second year students

00:01:28.280 --> 00:01:30.770
who have not had as much of an opportunity

00:01:30.770 --> 00:01:33.130
to spend time on campus in our museums,

00:01:33.130 --> 00:01:36.910
collections, libraries, research
centers, and institutes,

00:01:36.910 --> 00:01:40.200
and who have not had the
chance to meet the many people

00:01:40.200 --> 00:01:41.700
who steward these resources

00:01:41.700 --> 00:01:44.320
and allow us to navigate them as students,

00:01:44.320 --> 00:01:45.963
teachers, and scholars.

00:01:47.090 --> 00:01:49.310
But today's event is also a statement

00:01:49.310 --> 00:01:53.170
to show how research in the
humanities has been reactivated

00:01:53.170 --> 00:01:56.220
after the past 18 months
delayed our ability

00:01:56.220 --> 00:01:58.400
to be in archives and facilities,

00:01:58.400 --> 00:02:00.833
and face-to-face with our collaborators.

00:02:01.810 --> 00:02:03.780
I hope that this event
will be a celebration

00:02:03.780 --> 00:02:05.500
of the humanities at Yale,

00:02:05.500 --> 00:02:07.250
and that it will inspire many individuals

00:02:07.250 --> 00:02:10.333
to explore new directions
in their academic work.

00:02:11.840 --> 00:02:13.060
We'll begin momentarily

00:02:13.060 --> 00:02:14.880
with our first research presentation,

00:02:14.880 --> 00:02:17.530
but I want to remind
everyone that the info fair

00:02:17.530 --> 00:02:18.560
that will be taking place

00:02:18.560 --> 00:02:20.170
throughout the duration of today's event

00:02:20.170 --> 00:02:23.730
is just around the corner in room 134.

00:02:23.730 --> 00:02:26.420
And representatives from units
across the Yale's campus,

00:02:26.420 --> 00:02:29.370
including west campus are
there to answer questions

00:02:29.370 --> 00:02:31.570
about how you can take advantage
of all the opportunities

00:02:31.570 --> 00:02:35.133
for scholarly inquiry that
our university has to offer.

00:02:40.420 --> 00:02:43.040
I want to thank Taran and Zave,

00:02:43.040 --> 00:02:44.940
our McDougal fellows for all their help

00:02:44.940 --> 00:02:49.340
and Liz's help with putting
together today's symposium.

00:02:49.340 --> 00:02:53.130
And with that, I'll turn
to our first presenter.

00:02:53.130 --> 00:02:56.290
Minh Vu is a PhD student
in American studies

00:02:56.290 --> 00:02:58.270
and women's gender and sexuality studies.

00:02:58.270 --> 00:03:02.820
Minh's centers on relational
Afro Asian formations of race,

00:03:02.820 --> 00:03:06.150
cultural productions and
histories of solidarities.

00:03:06.150 --> 00:03:08.360
In particular, Minh examines water

00:03:08.360 --> 00:03:12.120
as an analytic connecting
histories of transatlantic slavery

00:03:12.120 --> 00:03:14.470
and transpacific imperialism.

00:03:14.470 --> 00:03:16.200
Minh also serves as a fellow

00:03:16.200 --> 00:03:18.380
for the Yale Prison Education Initiative

00:03:18.380 --> 00:03:20.430
for which they'll be talking about today.

00:03:21.630 --> 00:03:22.463
Welcome, Minh.

00:03:24.595 --> 00:03:27.595
(audience applauds)

00:03:31.300 --> 00:03:32.760
- So good morning, everyone.

00:03:32.760 --> 00:03:35.863
My name is Minh and I am a second year.

00:03:36.880 --> 00:03:39.850
So today I'm just gonna share
with you some information

00:03:39.850 --> 00:03:42.750
about a really special
program here at the university

00:03:42.750 --> 00:03:45.520
called the Yale Prison
Education Initiative.

00:03:45.520 --> 00:03:46.690
But before I begin,

00:03:46.690 --> 00:03:48.610
I just wanted to share
some quick acknowledgements

00:03:48.610 --> 00:03:51.540
and disclaimers to just
initiate the presentation

00:03:51.540 --> 00:03:54.450
with a tone of generosity
and collaboration.

00:03:54.450 --> 00:03:58.200
So first thank you so
much to Dr. Matthew Tanico

00:03:58.200 --> 00:03:59.620
for organizing the symposium

00:03:59.620 --> 00:04:01.970
and leading us in
shaping its intellectual,

00:04:01.970 --> 00:04:04.653
logistical, and programmatic contours.

00:04:05.530 --> 00:04:08.440
Next I wanted to index my deep gratitude

00:04:08.440 --> 00:04:10.040
for the YPEI community,

00:04:10.040 --> 00:04:13.060
first to the leadership team
composed of James Jetter

00:04:13.060 --> 00:04:15.240
who's the founder of the Dwight Hall Civic

00:04:15.240 --> 00:04:17.347
Allyship Initiative.

00:04:17.347 --> 00:04:19.410
Second to Vanessa Sume

00:04:19.410 --> 00:04:22.130
who is the new assistant director of YPEI,

00:04:22.130 --> 00:04:24.940
and last but not least Dr. Zelda Roland

00:04:24.940 --> 00:04:26.680
who's the founding director of YPEI

00:04:26.680 --> 00:04:30.920
as well as alum of Yale
College and Yale GSAS.

00:04:30.920 --> 00:04:32.920
Last but not least and most importantly,

00:04:34.180 --> 00:04:36.220
I am endlessly grateful to the students

00:04:36.220 --> 00:04:37.810
of the Yale Prison Education Initiative

00:04:37.810 --> 00:04:40.370
who I've been able to
be in deep conversation

00:04:40.370 --> 00:04:42.690
and community with since
my senior year here

00:04:42.690 --> 00:04:45.783
as an undergrad and now
in the graduate school.

00:04:46.970 --> 00:04:48.830
And through shared practice
of study of struggle,

00:04:48.830 --> 00:04:51.830
the students have completely appended

00:04:51.830 --> 00:04:54.910
how I come to move through and
underneath higher education,

00:04:54.910 --> 00:04:57.723
the classroom and more
broadly the university.

00:04:58.918 --> 00:05:00.780
The YPEI students and leadership team

00:05:00.780 --> 00:05:04.190
all could speak about
YPEI in higher education

00:05:04.190 --> 00:05:06.860
and present way more
robustly than I could.

00:05:06.860 --> 00:05:07.743
So by no means,

00:05:09.061 --> 00:05:12.120
am I gonna capture everything
in these 25 minutes,

00:05:12.120 --> 00:05:14.540
but I just wanted to share a
bit about my own involvement.

00:05:14.540 --> 00:05:16.950
So as to give interested
graduate students a brief glimpse

00:05:16.950 --> 00:05:17.893
into the program.

00:05:20.090 --> 00:05:21.530
Let's see.

00:05:21.530 --> 00:05:23.950
And yes, so related to
this idea of experience

00:05:23.950 --> 00:05:26.820
and expertise, this presentation
is a really casual one.

00:05:26.820 --> 00:05:29.560
I sort of give a broad
bird's eye view of YPEI

00:05:29.560 --> 00:05:31.210
that's marked by the dark blue sides,

00:05:31.210 --> 00:05:33.140
which were shared with me through Zelda.

00:05:33.140 --> 00:05:34.770
And then as I go into
the relevant programs

00:05:34.770 --> 00:05:38.020
I was involved with, I'll
dip into my own reflections,

00:05:38.020 --> 00:05:40.680
which are marked by the slides
with the white backdrop.

00:05:40.680 --> 00:05:41.760
And through this weaving,

00:05:41.760 --> 00:05:43.710
I hope to give like a
sort of informational

00:05:43.710 --> 00:05:46.360
as well as personal account
of higher ed in prison

00:05:46.360 --> 00:05:48.090
and its political, pedagogical,

00:05:48.090 --> 00:05:49.703
and programmatic possibilities.

00:05:51.770 --> 00:05:53.723
For some brief context,

00:05:55.512 --> 00:05:57.150
the Yale Prison Education Initiative

00:05:57.150 --> 00:05:58.820
is a program founded by Zelda

00:05:58.820 --> 00:05:59.860
run through Dwight Hall at Yale,

00:05:59.860 --> 00:06:01.340
which is the center for public service

00:06:01.340 --> 00:06:04.500
and social justice located at old campus.

00:06:04.500 --> 00:06:07.010
YPEI's main practices
include offering Yale

00:06:07.010 --> 00:06:09.860
and university of New
Haven courses in prison,

00:06:09.860 --> 00:06:11.930
connecting incarcerated
students with academic

00:06:11.930 --> 00:06:13.800
and educational resources.

00:06:13.800 --> 00:06:16.910
We're working with really
students and assisting

00:06:16.910 --> 00:06:18.440
and reentry through collaborations

00:06:18.440 --> 00:06:20.560
with local community organizations

00:06:20.560 --> 00:06:23.150
and most broadly advocating
for higher education in prison,

00:06:23.150 --> 00:06:25.113
both within Yale and also beyond.

00:06:26.440 --> 00:06:30.170
The program started offering
classes in the summer of 2018

00:06:30.170 --> 00:06:32.450
after four years of development funding

00:06:32.450 --> 00:06:34.420
and building partnerships
between the university

00:06:34.420 --> 00:06:37.980
and the Connecticut
Department of Corrections.

00:06:37.980 --> 00:06:40.310
These classes included
studies in creative writing,

00:06:40.310 --> 00:06:42.950
American literature, drawing and painting,

00:06:42.950 --> 00:06:44.780
and provided students with transferrable

00:06:44.780 --> 00:06:45.883
Yale College credit.

00:06:46.810 --> 00:06:48.010
In terms of location,

00:06:48.010 --> 00:06:50.060
as of now, YPEI works with students

00:06:50.060 --> 00:06:52.860
at the McDougal-Walker
Correctional Institution,

00:06:52.860 --> 00:06:55.890
which is a high and max
security level facility

00:06:55.890 --> 00:06:57.550
for adult men.

00:06:57.550 --> 00:07:01.333
MWCI is around a 50 minute
drive from New Haven.

00:07:03.326 --> 00:07:05.660
And then in terms of admissions
and class composition,

00:07:05.660 --> 00:07:08.870
the first sets of classes
had over 600 applications

00:07:08.870 --> 00:07:12.660
from adult men between
the ages of 23 and 49.

00:07:12.660 --> 00:07:15.550
In the constructing and
functioning of the YPEI classroom,

00:07:15.550 --> 00:07:18.200
there's no discriminatory consideration

00:07:18.200 --> 00:07:21.590
of any applicant's length of
sentence or type of conviction.

00:07:21.590 --> 00:07:23.660
Students are engaged as interlocutors

00:07:23.660 --> 00:07:26.410
and companions with a
broad variety of academic

00:07:26.410 --> 00:07:27.900
and extracurricular interests,

00:07:27.900 --> 00:07:30.640
just like non incarcerated
students here at Yale.

00:07:30.640 --> 00:07:33.083
And over the past three years,

00:07:33.980 --> 00:07:35.870
the program has grown tremendously

00:07:35.870 --> 00:07:38.680
through the collective work
of the YPEI leadership team,

00:07:38.680 --> 00:07:41.170
YPEI students, faculty
members, grad students,

00:07:41.170 --> 00:07:43.300
and undergrad volunteers.

00:07:43.300 --> 00:07:45.070
So as you can see, since 2018,

00:07:45.070 --> 00:07:47.580
there have been over
194 unique enrollments

00:07:47.580 --> 00:07:50.270
for 43 incarcerated students

00:07:50.270 --> 00:07:52.650
and over 70 faculty
members and grad students,

00:07:52.650 --> 00:07:55.170
as well as over a hundred
undergraduate volunteers

00:07:56.120 --> 00:07:58.290
from all over the university

00:07:58.290 --> 00:08:00.670
who have been involved in
extending on-campus research

00:08:00.670 --> 00:08:03.440
and resources from Yale
to McDougall-Walker.

00:08:03.440 --> 00:08:05.743
And just briefly as these numbers show,

00:08:06.760 --> 00:08:09.900
YPEI has also been really generative

00:08:09.900 --> 00:08:11.940
in terms of blurring
the rigid distinctions

00:08:11.940 --> 00:08:15.300
between faculty graduate
students and undergrads,

00:08:15.300 --> 00:08:17.860
because the initiative has
been a really rigorous space

00:08:17.860 --> 00:08:21.070
where all of these different
realms of the university work.

00:08:21.070 --> 00:08:22.760
They work collectively
in bringing coursework

00:08:22.760 --> 00:08:24.193
and resources to students.

00:08:28.280 --> 00:08:30.450
On the second bullet point,

00:08:30.450 --> 00:08:31.560
as I mentioned at the beginning,

00:08:31.560 --> 00:08:34.370
I first started my involvement with YPEI

00:08:34.370 --> 00:08:35.680
as an undergrad here.

00:08:35.680 --> 00:08:38.260
And one of my most memorable
experiences during this interim

00:08:38.260 --> 00:08:40.030
was getting to work with professors

00:08:40.030 --> 00:08:41.810
Daniel Hosin, Leah Miracol,

00:08:41.810 --> 00:08:44.070
Lisa Lowe and Roderick Ferguson

00:08:44.070 --> 00:08:46.963
through their co-taught
intro to ERNM class.

00:08:48.110 --> 00:08:49.800
And from helping them build their syllabus

00:08:49.800 --> 00:08:52.380
to running supplementary
discussion sections

00:08:52.380 --> 00:08:54.260
and even doing those 50 minute drives

00:08:54.260 --> 00:08:56.370
to McDougall-Walker with the faculty,

00:08:56.370 --> 00:09:00.590
I would argue that YPEI makes
an intervention internally

00:09:00.590 --> 00:09:01.840
within Yale as well,

00:09:01.840 --> 00:09:04.590
because it organically
and deliberately provokes

00:09:04.590 --> 00:09:06.460
collaborations across disciplines,

00:09:06.460 --> 00:09:08.193
departments and job titles.

00:09:13.060 --> 00:09:14.880
And this slide just further elaborates

00:09:14.880 --> 00:09:17.110
upon the earlier points that I shared

00:09:17.110 --> 00:09:19.240
about YPEI student emissions.

00:09:19.240 --> 00:09:22.340
It's important to underscore
that YPEI students

00:09:22.340 --> 00:09:24.330
or Yale students taking Yale classes,

00:09:24.330 --> 00:09:27.490
taking existing courses
offered to undergraduates here

00:09:27.490 --> 00:09:28.900
with the same course rigor,

00:09:28.900 --> 00:09:31.260
through to the best of our ability,

00:09:31.260 --> 00:09:34.250
similar assignments,
assessment standards, workload,

00:09:34.250 --> 00:09:38.000
and contact hours with peers,
teaching fellows, and faculty,

00:09:38.000 --> 00:09:39.120
whether through office hours,

00:09:39.120 --> 00:09:40.670
studio time for art classes

00:09:40.670 --> 00:09:42.680
and additional academic programming,

00:09:42.680 --> 00:09:46.030
such as workshops and lectures
from visiting professors

00:09:46.030 --> 00:09:47.123
and practitioners.

00:09:50.460 --> 00:09:53.540
And just continuing
this chronology of YPEI,

00:09:53.540 --> 00:09:57.300
the COVID 19 pandemic presented
a plethora of problems

00:09:57.300 --> 00:09:59.860
and interruptions to student learning.

00:09:59.860 --> 00:10:01.810
On March 12th, 2020,

00:10:01.810 --> 00:10:03.820
the Connecticut department of corrections

00:10:03.820 --> 00:10:06.260
suspended all outside
programming and visitations,

00:10:06.260 --> 00:10:10.070
including from students,
friends, and family members.

00:10:10.070 --> 00:10:13.280
But nonetheless, YPEI
worked both experimentally

00:10:13.280 --> 00:10:16.040
and openly to maintain our
commitment to the students

00:10:16.040 --> 00:10:19.140
by sending and receiving
assignments by snail mail.

00:10:19.140 --> 00:10:19.990
And through these letters,

00:10:19.990 --> 00:10:22.620
we were able to foster
students' independent studies

00:10:22.620 --> 00:10:24.480
and workshops via correspondence

00:10:24.480 --> 00:10:26.760
with the faculty and students.

00:10:26.760 --> 00:10:28.610
Some of these epistolary programs

00:10:28.610 --> 00:10:30.377
included a discussion section on

00:10:30.377 --> 00:10:32.920
"Race, Religion, and American Empire"

00:10:32.920 --> 00:10:36.460
by then PhD candidate, Victoria Baena.

00:10:36.460 --> 00:10:39.500
Our reading group on "Black
Marxism" by professor Ferguson

00:10:39.500 --> 00:10:41.670
and a creative writing
workshop organized by me

00:10:41.670 --> 00:10:43.640
and two other student fellows.

00:10:43.640 --> 00:10:45.150
And through these remote engagements,

00:10:45.150 --> 00:10:47.830
the YPEI students were able
to take all of the materials

00:10:47.830 --> 00:10:49.300
as starting points to facilitate

00:10:49.300 --> 00:10:50.610
their own in-person seminars

00:10:50.610 --> 00:10:52.810
and discussion groups
during the quarantine.

00:10:53.990 --> 00:10:56.010
In terms of the creative writing workshop,

00:10:56.010 --> 00:10:58.730
which I was involved
with as a first year here

00:10:58.730 --> 00:11:00.970
in the PhD program.

00:11:00.970 --> 00:11:02.820
I worked with two undergrad fellows,

00:11:02.820 --> 00:11:06.570
Nona and Gabby who really
spearheaded the program.

00:11:06.570 --> 00:11:09.640
And we invited a broad range of writers,

00:11:09.640 --> 00:11:12.360
artists and academics across the country

00:11:12.360 --> 00:11:15.940
to curate their own packets of
creative writing reflections,

00:11:15.940 --> 00:11:19.330
readings and response questions
to send to the students.

00:11:19.330 --> 00:11:21.110
Some of these writers
were on the last slide,

00:11:21.110 --> 00:11:24.820
but they included Franny Choi, Chen Chen,

00:11:24.820 --> 00:11:26.400
Alexander Chee, Briallen Hopper,

00:11:26.400 --> 00:11:28.403
Tavia Nyong'o, and Sunny Xiang.

00:11:29.420 --> 00:11:31.330
And after the students
received the packets,

00:11:31.330 --> 00:11:33.530
they respond to some of
the suggested prompts

00:11:33.530 --> 00:11:38.100
and sent back pieces of poetry,
nonfiction, fiction, memoir,

00:11:38.100 --> 00:11:40.970
and other creative writing
texts to be workshopped

00:11:40.970 --> 00:11:42.973
by student editors here at Yale.

00:11:43.870 --> 00:11:46.700
And even some of the pieces
ended up being published

00:11:46.700 --> 00:11:50.353
on some on-campus publications
that I'll show later.

00:11:51.190 --> 00:11:53.580
I won't bog you down with too
many more of these details

00:11:53.580 --> 00:11:55.110
about the contents or logistics,

00:11:55.110 --> 00:11:58.203
but you can visit the website
at the top to learn more.

00:12:01.250 --> 00:12:02.670
And now with classes in-person,

00:12:02.670 --> 00:12:03.900
there are versions of the workshop

00:12:03.900 --> 00:12:06.423
that are running in the fall as well.

00:12:07.700 --> 00:12:10.390
So then moving away from information

00:12:10.390 --> 00:12:11.630
and instead towards reflection,

00:12:11.630 --> 00:12:14.330
I just wanted to share these
two quotes from two scholars

00:12:14.330 --> 00:12:16.710
who have thought about
the necessity of art

00:12:16.710 --> 00:12:19.030
and of letter writing
as genres of intimacy

00:12:19.030 --> 00:12:21.700
that facilitate alternative
conceptions of space,

00:12:21.700 --> 00:12:24.670
of shared study and of
community formation.

00:12:24.670 --> 00:12:27.667
In the top left, Nicole
Fleetwood in her latest book,

00:12:27.667 --> 00:12:30.680
"Marking time writes, quote, art,

00:12:30.680 --> 00:12:33.230
and by art, she means of all mediums,

00:12:33.230 --> 00:12:34.880
creates relational possibilities

00:12:34.880 --> 00:12:36.950
that disrupt the mandate of the prison.

00:12:36.950 --> 00:12:39.090
Such artistic practices
radically challenge

00:12:39.090 --> 00:12:41.680
the impenetrability of prison.

00:12:41.680 --> 00:12:42.900
In the bottom right,

00:12:42.900 --> 00:12:45.380
Orasanmi Barton writes that letter writing

00:12:45.380 --> 00:12:47.080
within and beyond prisons

00:12:47.080 --> 00:12:49.050
functions as a form of community building,

00:12:49.050 --> 00:12:50.740
grassroots, intelligence gathering,

00:12:50.740 --> 00:12:52.863
collective theorization, and mutual aid.

00:12:53.970 --> 00:12:57.230
So resonant in these two quotes
that I just wanted to share

00:12:57.230 --> 00:13:00.980
is this idea of slipperiness
and how art and letter writing

00:13:00.980 --> 00:13:04.330
are collective practices that
trouble the architectures

00:13:04.330 --> 00:13:06.460
of borders, walls, and locks.

00:13:06.460 --> 00:13:08.360
And throughout the letter exchanges

00:13:08.360 --> 00:13:10.303
during the pandemic within YPEI,

00:13:11.420 --> 00:13:13.620
there was like this
sort of anxiety about...

00:13:15.260 --> 00:13:17.970
Like a reassurance about the uncertainties

00:13:17.970 --> 00:13:19.540
that snail mail presents.

00:13:19.540 --> 00:13:21.800
So one of the barriers that we faced

00:13:21.800 --> 00:13:23.760
when adapting to the
pandemic was that sending

00:13:23.760 --> 00:13:27.150
and receiving physical
letters took up to two weeks,

00:13:27.150 --> 00:13:28.770
given travel time, oversight,

00:13:28.770 --> 00:13:31.083
and processing within the prison.

00:13:31.083 --> 00:13:33.210
And this delay in timing
basically meant that

00:13:33.210 --> 00:13:36.940
there was a sort of disjuncture
between sender and receiver

00:13:36.940 --> 00:13:38.650
'cause the New Haven would have to work.

00:13:38.650 --> 00:13:40.050
Proactively while in advance,

00:13:40.050 --> 00:13:42.350
whereas the students in Suffield,

00:13:42.350 --> 00:13:45.790
I had to work more
retroactively given the delays.

00:13:45.790 --> 00:13:46.650
And when letters are sent,

00:13:46.650 --> 00:13:51.010
there's this kind of like
ambiguous sense of like,

00:13:51.010 --> 00:13:53.030
did it get lost in the mail?

00:13:53.030 --> 00:13:54.750
Will the receiver reply?

00:13:54.750 --> 00:13:56.380
When will I write back?

00:13:56.380 --> 00:13:59.380
And despite these logistical
and emotional anxieties

00:13:59.380 --> 00:14:01.000
which were compounded by the pandemic

00:14:01.000 --> 00:14:04.160
and its mandate for like
separation and quarantining.

00:14:04.160 --> 00:14:05.900
The YPEI community was still able

00:14:05.900 --> 00:14:08.580
to be in an intimate
network of letter exchanges

00:14:08.580 --> 00:14:09.563
for around a year.

00:14:11.030 --> 00:14:14.590
And lastly, in these two
quotes, Fleetwood and Burton,

00:14:14.590 --> 00:14:16.120
are speaking specifically about art

00:14:16.120 --> 00:14:18.540
and letter writing in the
context of the prison.

00:14:18.540 --> 00:14:20.390
But I would even extend their analysis

00:14:20.390 --> 00:14:21.980
to the university too,

00:14:21.980 --> 00:14:24.450
as these letters were
crucial modes of community,

00:14:24.450 --> 00:14:26.500
even for those here in New Haven

00:14:26.500 --> 00:14:29.803
and our shift to purely Zoom.

00:14:31.930 --> 00:14:35.420
So moving onto the quite recent.

00:14:35.420 --> 00:14:38.670
In April, 2021, YPEI
received a three-year grant

00:14:38.670 --> 00:14:40.860
from the Andrew Mellon Foundation.

00:14:40.860 --> 00:14:44.820
And this $1.5 million grant
has been really pivotal

00:14:44.820 --> 00:14:46.650
in allowing YPEI students,

00:14:46.650 --> 00:14:48.490
both current and prospective

00:14:48.490 --> 00:14:50.900
to earn a two year associate's degrees

00:14:50.900 --> 00:14:52.470
from the university of New Haven

00:14:52.470 --> 00:14:56.250
by taking courses taught by
both Yale and UNH faculty

00:14:56.250 --> 00:14:57.660
and grad students.

00:14:57.660 --> 00:15:00.830
So again, we see this sort of
like increasing slipperiness

00:15:00.830 --> 00:15:02.980
between the university and the prison

00:15:02.980 --> 00:15:05.670
as faculty and graduate
students across colleges

00:15:05.670 --> 00:15:06.780
begin collaborating.

00:15:06.780 --> 00:15:09.430
And as incarcerated students
begin acquiring degrees,

00:15:10.320 --> 00:15:13.160
and I would argue are still reserved

00:15:13.160 --> 00:15:15.450
for the incarcerated populations.

00:15:15.450 --> 00:15:17.740
And since this Mellon grant,

00:15:17.740 --> 00:15:22.410
YPEI has been able to offer
these in-person courses

00:15:22.410 --> 00:15:24.460
this past summer and this ongoing fall

00:15:24.460 --> 00:15:25.640
through partnerships with Yale

00:15:25.640 --> 00:15:27.390
and the university of New Haven.

00:15:27.390 --> 00:15:29.930
So in addition to the
creative writing workshop,

00:15:29.930 --> 00:15:32.360
this first course on the left,

00:15:32.360 --> 00:15:37.360
English 120 was my next
main engagement with YPEI.

00:15:37.760 --> 00:15:40.340
But this time it was
in a more official role

00:15:40.340 --> 00:15:43.050
as a teaching fellow funded
through YPEI's partnership

00:15:43.050 --> 00:15:44.343
with Yale summer session.

00:15:46.520 --> 00:15:50.150
And this transition from as
undergraduate student volunteer

00:15:50.150 --> 00:15:53.230
to graduate student teacher has
been really formative for me

00:15:53.230 --> 00:15:55.330
in terms of reshaping my commitments

00:15:55.330 --> 00:15:59.090
and also on commitments to
pedagogy and higher education

00:15:59.090 --> 00:16:01.290
as they are structured
the way they are now.

00:16:02.480 --> 00:16:03.557
So this quote here,

00:16:03.557 --> 00:16:06.410
"a real togetherness was developing"

00:16:06.410 --> 00:16:08.670
is from Angela Davis' autobiography

00:16:08.670 --> 00:16:12.810
where she reflects on the
necessity of a pedagogy

00:16:12.810 --> 00:16:15.630
while recounting her
time being incarcerated

00:16:15.630 --> 00:16:17.930
in the New York Women's
House of Detention,

00:16:17.930 --> 00:16:20.160
but still continuing her
professor aerial work,

00:16:20.160 --> 00:16:22.760
but through the lens of
a jailhouse pedagogy.

00:16:22.760 --> 00:16:25.250
And the reason why I invoke this quote,

00:16:25.250 --> 00:16:28.630
this idea of togetherness as
an active and ongoing verb

00:16:29.720 --> 00:16:33.260
is because the English 120
creative writing course,

00:16:33.260 --> 00:16:35.190
which is one of the first classes,

00:16:35.190 --> 00:16:37.270
the new cohort of YPEI students took.

00:16:37.270 --> 00:16:40.350
And one of the intro
classes that a lot of Yale

00:16:40.350 --> 00:16:43.630
first years take was not just
a standard seminar discussion,

00:16:43.630 --> 00:16:46.543
but also had peer review
workshops at least once a week.

00:16:47.980 --> 00:16:49.910
And as someone who took
creative writing workshops

00:16:49.910 --> 00:16:51.720
here as an undergrad,

00:16:51.720 --> 00:16:54.550
I noticed that the sort
of practice of community

00:16:54.550 --> 00:16:57.490
took on extremely different
shapes between the classrooms

00:16:57.490 --> 00:16:59.333
at Southfield versus in New Haven.

00:17:00.170 --> 00:17:01.880
So for the YPEI students,

00:17:01.880 --> 00:17:06.400
togetherness manifests as a
form of like shared procarity

00:17:06.400 --> 00:17:08.620
and collective study and struggle,

00:17:08.620 --> 00:17:11.730
and workshops where it
spaces of honest and kind,

00:17:11.730 --> 00:17:14.273
but also rigorous and
difficult conversations.

00:17:16.853 --> 00:17:18.700
And New Haven, I think
sometimes have tinges

00:17:18.700 --> 00:17:22.410
of professionalization, socialization,

00:17:22.410 --> 00:17:23.640
and therefore some shieldedness

00:17:23.640 --> 00:17:26.190
that I think can get in the
way of learning together

00:17:26.190 --> 00:17:28.410
in a collective and vulnerable fashion.

00:17:28.410 --> 00:17:30.543
And just in sharing this anecdote,

00:17:31.450 --> 00:17:34.010
I just wanted to index
that teaching in YPEI

00:17:34.010 --> 00:17:35.250
has allowed me to garner

00:17:35.250 --> 00:17:38.250
not just a wonderful community
of friends and interlocutors.

00:17:40.635 --> 00:17:42.650
They're not tools, but like
more so like principles,

00:17:42.650 --> 00:17:45.680
like guiding principles
to bring into the classes

00:17:45.680 --> 00:17:46.680
here at Yale buildings

00:17:46.680 --> 00:17:49.653
as I continue to teach and
enter my third and fourth years.

00:17:51.580 --> 00:17:54.560
So I just wanted to give three quotes

00:17:54.560 --> 00:17:56.850
that we can put in
conversation with each other,

00:17:56.850 --> 00:17:58.830
all from different perspectives.

00:17:58.830 --> 00:18:03.300
So this quote here is a
YPEI student testimony.

00:18:03.300 --> 00:18:05.357
I'll just read bits of it.

00:18:05.357 --> 00:18:06.977
"I've taken strides educationally

00:18:06.977 --> 00:18:08.857
"that were once unfathomable,

00:18:08.857 --> 00:18:12.807
"let alone unreachable, not
only because of accessibility,

00:18:12.807 --> 00:18:14.827
"but because of the
mental, emotional, social,

00:18:14.827 --> 00:18:16.563
"and intellectual standstill.

00:18:18.967 --> 00:18:20.647
"My brain and heart have been stored

00:18:20.647 --> 00:18:22.447
"that YPEI has unlocked.

00:18:22.447 --> 00:18:24.737
"The deans professors,
students, former students,

00:18:24.737 --> 00:18:25.997
"and volunteers that I've been blessed

00:18:25.997 --> 00:18:28.767
"to come into contact with
have treated me with inequality

00:18:28.767 --> 00:18:29.757
"that has been foreign to me

00:18:29.757 --> 00:18:31.807
"since my departure from the free world."

00:18:33.350 --> 00:18:36.150
This is a reflection from a
graduate student instructor.

00:18:37.710 --> 00:18:38.543
They say,

00:18:38.543 --> 00:18:40.837
"I have grown increasingly
convinced that this initiative

00:18:40.837 --> 00:18:44.657
"embodies the very best of what
Yale is and inspires to be:

00:18:44.657 --> 00:18:46.687
"a center of innovative
teaching and research

00:18:46.687 --> 00:18:49.037
"that seeks to educate and
learn from the brightest,

00:18:49.037 --> 00:18:51.707
"most creative students,
wherever they may be."

00:18:52.810 --> 00:18:56.610
And then this third slide is a reflection

00:18:56.610 --> 00:18:59.630
from a YPEI faculty member, Rod Ferguson,

00:18:59.630 --> 00:19:00.853
and he writes that,

00:19:03.310 --> 00:19:06.340
one of the stand up and
singularly teaching experiences

00:19:06.340 --> 00:19:08.110
for him has been the opportunity to teach

00:19:08.110 --> 00:19:10.393
in the Yale Prison Education Initiative.

00:19:11.240 --> 00:19:14.810
YPEI students have exacting
standards as readers

00:19:14.810 --> 00:19:15.890
and interlocutors,

00:19:15.890 --> 00:19:17.990
they're not just going to
accept anything that you say

00:19:17.990 --> 00:19:19.070
because you're the authority

00:19:19.070 --> 00:19:21.450
or because the text was
written by so-and-so.

00:19:21.450 --> 00:19:23.503
They're also incredibly generous.

00:19:24.630 --> 00:19:28.190
So in giving you these
three faces of YPEI,

00:19:28.190 --> 00:19:30.328
I think they all prompt revisitation

00:19:30.328 --> 00:19:32.240
and also a reconsideration

00:19:32.240 --> 00:19:33.900
of the purpose of higher education

00:19:33.900 --> 00:19:37.803
and our own roles within it
as members of the university.

00:19:38.700 --> 00:19:40.850
So here's Yale College's
mission statement.

00:19:40.850 --> 00:19:43.810
And I just underlined some phrases

00:19:43.810 --> 00:19:47.023
that I think could be further discussed.

00:19:48.530 --> 00:19:51.630
I think the Yale college mission statement

00:19:51.630 --> 00:19:54.590
in context of the Yale
Prison Education Initiative,

00:19:54.590 --> 00:19:57.630
but also higher ed in
prison programs beyond

00:19:58.580 --> 00:20:01.560
prompt us to think about who is excluded,

00:20:01.560 --> 00:20:03.660
but also included from
this mission statement

00:20:03.660 --> 00:20:06.623
and what sort of possibilities
lie in the future.

00:20:09.470 --> 00:20:12.550
And yes, these are just some
slides of the YPEI students

00:20:12.550 --> 00:20:15.260
collaborations with on-campus publications

00:20:15.260 --> 00:20:16.700
during the creative writing workshop.

00:20:16.700 --> 00:20:20.950
On the left is the spring
2020 issue of the lit mag,

00:20:20.950 --> 00:20:23.460
where we had some students
publish their poetry

00:20:23.460 --> 00:20:26.830
alongside Yale students here in New Haven.

00:20:26.830 --> 00:20:29.530
In the middle is a YDN article.

00:20:29.530 --> 00:20:32.230
It was a special issue where
they invited by YPEI students

00:20:32.230 --> 00:20:37.200
to send some pieces related
to their experiences in YPEI.

00:20:37.200 --> 00:20:41.050
And on the right is this
awesome multimedia project

00:20:41.050 --> 00:20:44.090
that one of our students
had did with the binary key.

00:20:44.090 --> 00:20:45.810
I write book and manuscript library,

00:20:45.810 --> 00:20:49.763
and they published a mini exhibit
with writing and drawings.

00:20:51.650 --> 00:20:55.700
So in terms of opportunities
and ways to get involved,

00:20:55.700 --> 00:20:58.100
you can learn more about
it at the fair next door,

00:20:58.100 --> 00:21:01.293
but some partnerships for student support.

00:21:02.150 --> 00:21:03.990
Together we work with the Poorvu Center

00:21:03.990 --> 00:21:05.050
for Teaching and Learning

00:21:05.050 --> 00:21:07.140
to bring in academic strategies, mentors,

00:21:07.140 --> 00:21:09.780
and writing partners to the students.

00:21:09.780 --> 00:21:12.523
We also work with the
Yale University Library,

00:21:13.500 --> 00:21:16.730
sort of bring archival and
textbook resources to students

00:21:16.730 --> 00:21:20.180
as they do their research
papers and final projects.

00:21:20.180 --> 00:21:25.180
There's a YUL-YPEI student
coordinator position

00:21:25.510 --> 00:21:27.400
that will be opening next spring.

00:21:27.400 --> 00:21:29.300
And it's a paid student position,

00:21:29.300 --> 00:21:30.730
and it's ideally for a grad student

00:21:30.730 --> 00:21:33.630
interested in library
services and programming,

00:21:33.630 --> 00:21:36.853
and yes, TFs and accessibility
resources as well.

00:21:37.790 --> 00:21:40.370
And here are some, what are they called?

00:21:40.370 --> 00:21:44.290
QR codes that you can
scan if you're interested

00:21:44.290 --> 00:21:45.950
in volunteering.

00:21:45.950 --> 00:21:49.189
Thank you so much and happy
to answer any questions.

00:21:49.189 --> 00:21:52.189
(audience applauds)

00:22:00.472 --> 00:22:01.389
Sure, yeah.

00:22:09.170 --> 00:22:11.966
- [Man] I love the way
you talked about time

00:22:11.966 --> 00:22:14.020
throughout the pandemic,
then there's better writing.

00:22:14.020 --> 00:22:15.770
I think the academic calendar

00:22:15.770 --> 00:22:17.910
is such an interesting take on time.

00:22:17.910 --> 00:22:19.850
Has that impacted at all

00:22:19.850 --> 00:22:23.610
or have you thought at all about how

00:22:24.660 --> 00:22:26.740
the university might function differently

00:22:26.740 --> 00:22:28.650
if we thought about time differently

00:22:28.650 --> 00:22:32.675
with the possibilities that
we've been able to do now,

00:22:32.675 --> 00:22:35.600
thinking about time (mumbles).

00:22:35.600 --> 00:22:40.054
- Yeah, that's a really helpful question.

00:22:40.054 --> 00:22:43.210
I think the way time
operates for a classroom

00:22:43.210 --> 00:22:45.330
at McDougall-Walker versus time

00:22:45.330 --> 00:22:48.530
at a classroom here in a Yale
building is really different.

00:22:48.530 --> 00:22:52.160
I think time at McDougall is really quick

00:22:52.160 --> 00:22:54.450
and there are a lot more logistical issues

00:22:54.450 --> 00:22:55.283
that come with it.

00:22:55.283 --> 00:22:57.920
So for example, if a
student wants to write

00:22:57.920 --> 00:23:00.300
a research paper that
combines archival sources

00:23:00.300 --> 00:23:02.420
from a Yale library or secondary resources

00:23:02.420 --> 00:23:03.700
from a Yale library,

00:23:03.700 --> 00:23:08.130
that requires them to
submit a research request

00:23:08.130 --> 00:23:09.840
through our research requests network,

00:23:09.840 --> 00:23:12.990
that then has to shuttle
through a Yale undergraduate

00:23:12.990 --> 00:23:16.351
who then looks up some of
the relevant literature

00:23:16.351 --> 00:23:17.900
for the student to then send back in.

00:23:17.900 --> 00:23:20.630
And if you think about the time to go back

00:23:20.630 --> 00:23:23.400
between the prison and here is 50 minutes.

00:23:23.400 --> 00:23:27.840
So in that case, you can
immediately see the discrepancy

00:23:27.840 --> 00:23:29.050
and asymmetry that takes place

00:23:29.050 --> 00:23:32.110
in terms of research for a YPEI undergrad.

00:23:32.110 --> 00:23:34.080
Whereas like here at Yale, undergrads,

00:23:34.080 --> 00:23:36.770
grad students, faculty, it's our best,

00:23:36.770 --> 00:23:39.130
and you can get something
immediately through your laptop

00:23:39.130 --> 00:23:42.030
and through internet
and through your net ID.

00:23:42.030 --> 00:23:47.030
So I think there are a lot
of those logistical things

00:23:48.950 --> 00:23:51.170
that like a lot of us
here can take for granted

00:23:51.170 --> 00:23:52.067
when doing our research.

00:23:52.067 --> 00:23:54.390
The YPEI students have to overcome

00:23:54.390 --> 00:23:56.870
and think about as they
build their research

00:23:56.870 --> 00:23:57.863
and begin writing.

00:24:04.308 --> 00:24:08.808
- [Woman] Well, I actually (mumbles).

00:24:17.674 --> 00:24:21.565
It seems like there is something that

00:24:21.565 --> 00:24:24.898
shows destruction in everything they do.

00:24:26.515 --> 00:24:30.848
Everything is structured (mumbles).

00:24:36.323 --> 00:24:41.218
If you can see some of those
people in those positions.

00:24:41.218 --> 00:24:45.099
- Sorry, the lawnmower is really loud.

00:24:45.099 --> 00:24:48.432
- [Woman] So when you speak
of the level of initiative,

00:24:48.432 --> 00:24:53.432
then what motivates these (mumbles)

00:24:56.023 --> 00:25:01.023
in a normal situation how
you leverage (indistinct).

00:25:06.420 --> 00:25:10.310
- The question was how do the
incarcerated YPEI students

00:25:11.580 --> 00:25:13.900
take sort of the initiative
to attend classes

00:25:13.900 --> 00:25:16.830
within a sort of like
extremely structured day

00:25:17.860 --> 00:25:20.630
as the prison sort of organizes time

00:25:20.630 --> 00:25:22.253
and movement for the students?

00:25:27.472 --> 00:25:29.610
YPIE isn't just about classes for example,

00:25:29.610 --> 00:25:30.520
it's not just like,

00:25:30.520 --> 00:25:31.890
you do a class and you leave,

00:25:31.890 --> 00:25:34.750
it's all about different types
of other programming as well.

00:25:34.750 --> 00:25:37.680
Like we bring in visitors and lecturers

00:25:37.680 --> 00:25:38.840
who speak about their work.

00:25:38.840 --> 00:25:42.480
We have open study halls for
students to come together

00:25:43.590 --> 00:25:46.133
and talk about their own research.

00:25:47.348 --> 00:25:48.920
We also run workshops like
the creative writing workshop.

00:25:48.920 --> 00:25:51.630
We bring it in academic strategies mentors

00:25:51.630 --> 00:25:52.463
and writing partners.

00:25:52.463 --> 00:25:55.040
And that's because we want to create

00:25:56.410 --> 00:25:57.850
as comprehensive as we can

00:25:57.850 --> 00:25:59.850
of an undergraduate experience for them.

00:26:02.470 --> 00:26:07.470
YPEI is just a great way to get around

00:26:08.000 --> 00:26:10.620
the hyper organization of the prison,

00:26:10.620 --> 00:26:13.720
and think very openly and collectively

00:26:13.720 --> 00:26:17.360
about various political and intellectual

00:26:18.280 --> 00:26:20.030
things they've been thinking about.

00:26:21.920 --> 00:26:25.300
The students come from all
regions of the prison as well.

00:26:25.300 --> 00:26:28.550
So there's that sort of like
larger spatial divisions

00:26:28.550 --> 00:26:30.600
that we also have to think about as well.

